Fields

Every name that can appear in an expect file, what it holds, and which
kinds carry it. The other documents describe the objects

The corpus is the authority, not this file. The names in use are

    awk '{print $1}' test/*/*/expect | sort -u

and what they hold is written here.


Value forms

        hex        a byte string, lower case, no separators, never empty
        decimal    a count or a length, no sign, no padding
        keyword    one word from a set fixed per field, listed below
        -          absent, empty, or not determined

The second column below is the forms one field admits.

The three meanings of "-" are told apart by the field, not by the
value. An optional part of a packet that is not present is "-". An
empty byte string is "-", because hex cannot spell one and a name
followed by nothing is a line that cannot be told from a truncated one.
A verdict that was never reached is "-", which is what happens when no
key agreement was possible; see doc/encryption.

Names are at most 18 characters, which is the column the value starts
in. A wider name would shift the value and two decoders that pad alike
would still compare equal, so both cmd/dump and tools/gen refuse one.


Echoed inputs

Where raw carries more than the object, because the object cannot be
read without it, expect echoes those lines: expect then holds every byte
of raw and the encode direction has something to rebuild from. Each is
the line of raw named in the kind's own document. These are all of them.

    advertised_hash    hex      resourceproof: the hash the receiver was advertised
    recipient_private  hex      encrypted: the 64-byte identity private key
    ratchet_private    hex, -   encrypted: the 32-byte ratchet scalar, or -
    link_request       hex      linkproof, linkdata: the request packet
    responder_private  hex      linkdata: the responder's 32-byte link scalar
    signer_public      hex      linkproof, proof: the signer's public key, 64 bytes, or 32 for a proof on a link
    proved_packet      hex      proof: the packet being proved
    private_key        hex      keyset, sign: the 64-byte identity private key
    group_key          hex      group: the 64-byte shared symmetric key
    netname            hex, -   ifac: the network name the key comes from
    netkey             hex, -   ifac: the passphrase it comes from


Header

Carried by every kind whose raw is a whole packet: announce, plain,
pathrequest, encrypted, linkrequest, linkproof, linkdata, proof. See
doc/packet.

    flags              hex      the flags byte, one byte
    header_type        decimal  1 or 2, the human-facing numbering
    context_flag       keyword  set, unset
    transport_type     keyword  broadcast, transport, relay, tunnel
    destination_type   keyword  single, group, plain, link
    packet_type        keyword  data, announce, linkrequest, proof
    hops               decimal  the hop count, below 128
    transport_id       hex, -   header 2 only, otherwise -
    destination_hash   hex      the address field, 16 bytes
    context            keyword, hex
                                none, resource, resource_adv,
                                resource_req, resource_hmu, resource_prf,
                                resource_icl, resource_rcl, request,
                                response, path_response, channel,
                                keepalive, link_identify, link_close,
                                link_proof, link_rtt, link_request_proof
    payload_length     decimal  bytes after the header

The address field is called destination_hash for every kind, and for
two kinds it is not a destination hash: a link request proof carries
the link id there and a delivery proof carries the first 16 bytes of a
packet hash. link_id_match and destination_match are the fields that
say so.


Identity and destination

    public_key         hex      64 bytes, X25519 half then Ed25519 half
    x25519_public      hex      32 bytes
    ed25519_public     hex      32 bytes
    x25519_private     hex      32 bytes, the raw scalar, unclamped
    ed25519_private    hex      32 bytes, an RFC 8032 seed
    identity_hash      hex, -   SHA-256(public_key)[0:16]; - when there is no identity, as a plain destination has none
    name               hex      the full destination name, utf-8 bytes
    app_name           hex      the first component
    aspect             hex, -   one component, repeated; - when empty
    name_hash          hex      SHA-256(name)[0:10]
    destination_hash   hex      SHA-256(name_hash || identity_hash)[0:16]

aspect is the one repeated name in the format. The number of lines is
the number of components after the first, and their order is the order
in the name.

identity_hash appears in six kinds and means the same thing in all of
them: the hash of a public key. In an announce it is derived from the
announced key; in an encrypted packet it is derived from the recipient
key and is the salt of the derivation. See doc/announce, doc/encryption.


Announce

See doc/announce.

    public_key         hex      the announced identity key
    name_hash          hex      10 bytes; the name itself is not sent
    random_hash        hex      10 bytes, 5 random then 5 of unix time
    ratchet            hex, -   32 bytes when the context flag is set
    signature          hex      64 bytes
    app_data           hex, -   everything after the signature
    identity_hash      hex      derived from public_key
    expected_hash      hex      SHA-256(name_hash || identity_hash)[0:16]
    destination_match  keyword  yes, no: expected_hash against the header
    signed_data        hex      the assembled material, app data before the signature rather than after it
    signature_valid    keyword  yes, no

signed_data is printed in full because assembling it is the most
likely reason for a valid announce to be rejected.


Plain

See doc/packet, section Plain destinations. There is no key in the
vector, because nothing is encrypted.

    plaintext_length   decimal  bytes of payload
    plaintext          hex, -   the payload; - when empty


Path request

See doc/packet, section Path requests. The payload of a plain packet
addressed to the path request destination.

    wanted_hash        hex      16 bytes, the destination asked after
    requester_id       hex, -   16 bytes, the transport instance asking
    tag                hex, -   the rest of the payload; - when absent
    unique_tag         hex, -   wanted_hash || tag[0:16]
    accepted           keyword  yes, no: whether the reference acts on it

Which of the three parts are present is decided by the payload length
alone. unique_tag is what the duplicate check is made against, and it
takes at most 16 bytes of tag, so it is shorter than wanted_hash || tag
whenever the tag is longer than that.


Token: encrypted packets, group packets and link data

See doc/encryption for the destination and group cases and doc/link for
the link case. The token is the same construction; only the key differs,
and for a group packet it is not derived at all.

    ephemeral_public   hex      encrypted only: 32 bytes, in the packet
    ratchet_public     hex, -   encrypted only: derived from ratchet_private
    link_id            hex      linkdata: 16 bytes, from the link request
    link_id_match      keyword  yes, no: link_id against the header
    encrypted          keyword  linkdata: yes, no; a keepalive is not
    iv                 hex      16 bytes
    ciphertext         hex      a multiple of 16 bytes
    hmac               hex      32 bytes, over iv || ciphertext
    shared_key         hex, -   the X25519 output, 32 bytes; group packets have none and carry no such field
    signing_key        hex, -   first half of the 64 derived bytes, or of group_key, which is not derived
    encryption_key     hex, -   second half
    hmac_valid         keyword, - yes, no
    plaintext_length   decimal, - bytes, or - if nothing was decrypted
    plaintext          hex, -   the bytes; - when empty or absent

The three key fields and hmac_valid are "-" together, and only
together: they mean no shared secret exists, which happens when the
peer public key has small order. See doc/encryption.

plaintext_length 0 with plaintext "-" is an empty plaintext.
plaintext_length "-" is a failure. The two are not the same and the
pair is what tells them apart.

A channel packet adds four fields, which decompose the plaintext rather
than the packet. See doc/link.

    msgtype            hex      2 bytes, which message type it carries
    sequence           decimal  0 to 65535, the envelope's own counter
    declared_length    decimal  the length the envelope states
    message            hex, -   everything after the six-byte envelope

declared_length is what the envelope says and not what it carries. The
reference writes the two to agree and never reads the field back, so a
decoder that trusts it and one that ignores it can only be told apart
by a packet the reference cannot produce. See doc/link.

An identify proof on a link adds four fields. See doc/link.

    identity_public    hex      64 bytes, the initiator's identity key
    identity_hash      hex      derived from it
    identity_signed    hex      link_id || identity_public
    identity_valid     keyword  yes, no

A request adds three fields and a response two, all of them elements of
a msgpack array. See doc/link.

    request_time       hex, -   8 bytes, an IEEE 754 double, big endian
    request_path_hash  hex, -   16 bytes, the truncated hash of the path
    request_data       hex, -   the request body
    request_id         hex, -   16 bytes, naming the request packet
    response_data      hex, -   the response body

request_time is recorded as its eight bytes rather than as a number.
It is the only floating point value anywhere in the corpus, and a
decimal would have to survive being written and read back to reproduce
raw.

request_data and response_data are "-" for both an absent value and an
empty one: msgpack writes nil and a zero-length byte string
differently, and this format spells both absences the same way. No
vector carries the second.

All five are "-" when the msgpack body could not be read that far, the
third meaning of the dash. No vector carries that either; see doc/link.


Resource

See doc/resource. Six of the seven contexts a transfer uses are link
data packets and decompose the plaintext; the seventh is a kind of its
own.

An advertisement carries eleven fields, in the order the reference
writes the map.

    transfer_size      decimal  bytes of encrypted data to be moved
    data_size          decimal  bytes before compression and encryption
    resource_parts     decimal  how many parts the data was cut into
    resource_hash      hex      32 bytes, over the data and a random hash
    resource_random    hex      4 bytes, so that equal data differs
    original_hash      hex      32 bytes, the first segment's hash
    segment_index      decimal  which segment this advertises
    total_segments     decimal  how many there are
    request_id         hex, -   the request this answers, when it is one
    resource_flags     hex      one byte of six flags; see doc/resource
    hashmap            hex      four bytes per part, as many as fit

A part adds no field of its own. It is not encrypted by the packet
layer and cannot be named without the advertisement; see doc/resource.

A part request adds four.

    hashmap_exhausted  keyword  yes, no
    last_map_hash      hex, -   4 bytes, present when exhausted
    requested_hashes   hex, -   four bytes per part asked for

A cancel from either end carries resource_hash and nothing else.

The proof is the kind resourceproof, and its raw carries the resource
hash the receiver was advertised, because the packet does not say what
it is proving beyond naming it.

    advertised_hash    hex      the echoed input above: the hash from the advertisement
    resource_proof     hex      32 bytes, SHA-256 of the data and the resource hash
    hash_match         keyword  yes, no: resource_hash against advertised_hash


Link request and link request proof

See doc/link.

    x25519_public      hex      32 bytes, per link, not an identity key
    ed25519_public     hex      linkrequest only: 32 bytes, per link
    signalling         hex, -   3 bytes, or - in the shorter form
    mode               keyword, hex aes256_cbc
    mtu                decimal, - 21 bits, or - when nothing was signalled
    link_id            hex      16 bytes, over the request with the signalling bytes trimmed
    link_id_match      keyword  linkproof: yes, no
    signature          hex      linkproof: 64 bytes
    signer_ed25519     hex      linkproof: the Ed25519 half of the destination identity, not of the link
    signed_data        hex      linkproof: link_id || x25519_public || signer_ed25519 || signalling
    signature_valid    keyword  yes, no

Three of the four parts of signed_data are not in the packet, which is
why the vector is given the request and the identity key.


Delivery proof

See doc/packet, section Proofs, and doc/link for the link case.

    form               keyword  implicit, explicit
    packet_hash        hex      32 bytes, computed from proved_packet
    proof_hash         hex, -   explicit form only: the hash it carries
    hash_match         keyword  yes, no; yes for the implicit form
    proof_destination  hex      packet_hash[0:16], the address it should have; not on a link
    destination_match  keyword  yes, no; not on a link
    link_id            hex      on a link only: the address it should have, taken from the proved packet
    link_id_match      keyword  on a link only: yes, no
    signature          hex      64 bytes, over packet_hash
    signer_ed25519     hex      32 bytes
    signature_valid    keyword  yes, no

A proof addressed to a link carries link_id and link_id_match where one
addressed to a destination carries proof_destination and
destination_match. destination_type says which, and signer_public is 32
bytes rather than 64 in that case, because only one key verifies it.


Interface access code

See doc/packet, section Interface access codes.

    netname            hex, -   the network name, utf-8 bytes
    netkey             hex, -   the passphrase, utf-8 bytes
    ifac_origin        hex      32 or 64 bytes, the two hashes
    ifac_key           hex      64 bytes, derived from ifac_origin
    ifac_size          decimal  bytes of access code; configuration
    frame_length       decimal  bytes of the whole frame
    ifac               hex      the access code the frame carries
    packet             hex      the packet recovered from the frame
    expected_ifac      hex      the code that packet should have produced
    ifac_valid         keyword  yes, no

netname and netkey are "-" when that half is not configured, and each
absent half is one that contributes nothing to ifac_origin. Neither can
be both configured and empty here: a configuration that sets neither
has no access code at all.


Signature and sign

See doc/identity.

    message_length     decimal  bytes of the signed message
    message_sha256     hex      32 bytes; the message is not in expect, which is why these two kinds are decode class
    signature          hex      64 bytes; an input for signature and an output for sign
    valid              keyword  signature only: yes, no

sign has no verdict field. The signature is the answer.


Rejection

An object that broke a rule carries the rule and the numbers behind it,
and no other fields. Naming the rule rather than the stage keeps two
decoders from agreeing by accident while failing for different reasons.

    invalid            keyword  short-header, hop-limit, short-payload, invalid-length, short-plaintext
    length             decimal  short-header: bytes present
    minimum_length     decimal  short-header, short-payload, short-plaintext: bytes needed
    payload_length     decimal  short-payload, invalid-length: bytes present
    hops               decimal  hop-limit: the count
    hop_limit          decimal  hop-limit: the threshold, 128
    accepted_length    decimal  invalid-length: the shorter accepted length
    signalled_length   decimal  invalid-length: the longer one
    implicit_length    decimal  invalid-length: proof, 64
    explicit_length    decimal  invalid-length: proof, 96

length, payload_length and hops are header field names as well. In a
rejection they are the evidence for the rule that was broken, and the
presence of invalid is what says which reading applies.

short-plaintext is the one rejection that leaves the fields before it
standing. The other four say the packet was not read; this one says the
packet was read whole and the plaintext inside it was too short for the
context byte that named it. The header, the token and the keys are
therefore all present and correct, and the vector is of the encode
class, which the others are not. The length is the plaintext_length
already printed above, so only the threshold is repeated. See
doc/resource.
